meta {
  name: 09 - Flip Task Block (CAUTION)
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

put {
  url: {{base_url}}/blocks
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{craft_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "{{test_block_id}}",
        "markdown": "> - [x] Commencé la candidature ?"
      }
    ]
  }
}

docs {
  WRITE test - mutates a real block. Validates wm_automation.craft.tracker's
  mark_started/mark_applied approach: PUT /blocks with the task block's id
  and its markdown changed from "[ ]" to "[x]" - only `markdown` is
  documented as writable for text blocks, taskInfo isn't, so this checks
  whether Craft re-derives taskInfo.state from the markdown on write.

  Pre-filled `test_block_id` default targets a REAL untouched offer -
  its "Commencé la candidature ?" checklist block, confirmed still
  todo as of the last full-document fetch. This is a real tracker entry,
  not a throwaway - Craft's docs flag this as production data, and this
  is only "safe" because it's reversible:

  1. Run this request.
  2. Run request 06 (or 08) again and check this block's taskInfo.state -
     did it flip to "done"?
  3. Revert immediately: re-run this same request with markdown changed
     back to "> - [ ] Commencé la candidature ?" (edit the body above),
     and confirm via 06/08 that it's back to "todo" - you haven't actually
     started that application, so leaving it marked done would be wrong
     data, not just a leftover test artifact.

  Only after a successful flip + revert should pipeline.py's
  mark_started/mark_applied calls (currently wrapped in a non-fatal
  try/except in prepare_application/finalize_application) be trusted.
}
